in belgium 23 people are going on trial  today in the city of bruges in  connection with the deaths of 39  vietnamese migrants in 2019 the migrants  were found dead in the back of a truck  near london several men including the  drivers have already been jailed in the  uk the trial targets the larger human  trafficking network that was smuggling  people people seeking a better life in  britain  for more let's bring in dw correspondent  terry schulz she's live in bruges for us  terry what are the charges being heard  today at the court  hello yes well in the courthouse behind  me prosecutors are describing the  belgian leg of an alleged trafficking  network stretching from vietnam to the  uk  they say that these 39 victims were  gathered in a safe house in brussels  then transported by car to france where  they were loaded into this refrigerated  container with no incoming oxygen that  container was then brought nearby where  i am now to the port of zebra get and  sent across to the uk of course the 39  people inside would not make it now the  23 defendants range from taxi drivers to  the accused ringleader of 45 year old  vietnamese man and i should say i just  spoke with his attorney who says his  client is innocent but he does face a  significant prison term  now these tragic deaths of the  vietnamese migrants happened in october  2019. let's have a look back at how this  tragedy unfolded  their journey ended here an industrial  estate east of london  in october 2019 british police  discovered the lifeless bodies of 39  vietnamese migrants in this trailer  truck  two of the victims were just 15 years  old  they'd each paid thousands to people  smugglers to cross from belgium to the  uk in hopes of starting a better life  instead packed inside a sealed container  for at least 12 hours they suffocated to  death  in the uk  seven men have already been given  lengthy prison sentences  the defendants on trial in bruges are  accused of operating a human trafficking  ring between belgium and the uk  the majority are themselves a vietnamese  descent  terri it's really horrible to imagine  the suffering that these people went  through what more can you tell us about  the victims  that's right we know from evidence at  the scene that as they realized they  were running out of air they tried to  poke holes in this container but it was  absolutely impenetrable and and it  wasn't possible at all to either do that  or alert anyone to open the container  and get them some air so as they realize  they were not going to survive the the  victims started sending messages to  their loved ones we know that one man  told his wife and children that that he  was sorry he wouldn't be able to take  care of them anymore but that he simply  couldn't breathe  one 26 year old woman sent a message  telling her mother that she loved her  and that she was sorry that her trip  abroad had failed but that she was now  dying now some of these family members  are due to share their stories with the  court  tough stuff to to hear there terry how  will this case proceed  the trial now in the in the building  behind me is due to take two days and  again with 23 defendants the the charges  range uh vastly from from you know  simply being a taxi driver who  transported some of these people to this  45 year old man who is really seen by  prosecutors as the man at the center of  the belgian leg of this this trafficking  network again his lawyer says he's  innocent but he does face up to 20 years  in prison and we should also point out  that investigations in the uk and in  vietnam have returned heavy prison  sentences for those determined to have  been involved so i would imagine that  his defense team is feeling a little bit  unsettled at the moment  terry thank you dw's terry schultz for  us in bruges  you
 

